; A real exchange, 2026-11-05: a human (@user, relaying by hand, messages drafted and
; validated by Claude Code) and Dola (dola.com), a third-party agent app that had only
; been given rulebook/aicl-skill.zip as a skill. The task: research the defense
; chemicals of broccoli. On the wire Dola mixed dense act codes with readable bodies
; ((K dola.4>user …), (T dola.6>user …)); this file is the canonical form.
;
; What it shows (all live, kept as specimens):
;  - A typed def (SPEC §6.1) works as a report schema for a chat agent: @user defined
;    defense-chemicals with :args [(vegetable symbol)] :ret (list term) and a gloss
;    describing the (compound …) report shape; Dola's delivery matches it exactly -
;    five compounds with :class/:role/:human/:cooking and nine structured :src entries.
;  - ok-for-ask: Dola first ACKNOWLEDGED the ask with (ok #dola.4) - 'ok' answers
;    do/def/sub, an ask wants tell/no/fail/clar. This error check exists because of
;    this exchange.
;  - The performer did not track its own promise: asked for status, it answered
;    {$s unknown} about the very request it had just acknowledged; the original ask
;    stays open (the re-ask #user.8 got the answer).
;  - Dola never sent ok for the two defs, yet used the vocabulary - hence the
;    unresolved-term warnings; the defs bind only once acknowledged.

(def #user.1 @user>@dola defense-chemicals :args [(vegetable symbol)] :gloss (nl en "the chemical compounds a vegetable produces to defend itself against herbivores, insects, fungi or pathogens. Report each compound as a form: (compound <name> :class <chemical class> :role (nl en why the plant makes it) :human (nl en effect on humans - benefit or harm, with typical amounts) :cooking (nl en how cooking or preparation changes it)) and give :src naming the sources you actually consulted, or (none)") :ret (list term))
(def #user.2 @user>@dola compound :args [(name symbol)] :gloss (nl en "one defense chemical in an answer to defense-chemicals: the name, its chemical class, and the nl notes described there"))
(ask #user.3 @user>@dola (defense-chemicals broccoli $c))
(ok #dola.4 @dola>@user ^user.3)
(ask #user.7 @user>@dola (status (ref #user.3) $s))
(tell #dola.5 @dola>@user ^user.7 {$s unknown})
(ask #user.8 @user>@dola (defense-chemicals broccoli $c))
(tell #dola.6 @dola>@user ^user.8 {$c [(compound glucoraphanin :class glucosinolate-aliphatic :cooking (nl en "Boiling leaches glucosinolates into the water (~40-58% loss) and heat above ~70C denatures myrosinase, sharply cutting sulforaphane formation. Short steaming preserves most; if myrosinase is destroyed, gut microbiota convert some glucoraphanin but less efficiently. Adding mustard seed powder (exogenous myrosinase) restores sulforaphane yield; cutting and resting ~40 min before cooking lets sulforaphane form first.") :human (nl en "Benefit: precursor of sulforaphane, which activates the Nrf2 antioxidant/detox response and is studied for cancer chemoprevention, anti-inflammation and cardiovascular protection. Typical mature florets ~0.7-1.3 umol/g fresh weight (~30-60 mg/100 g), highly cultivar- and season-dependent; sprouts and seeds ~10x higher. Harm: dietary amounts safe; concentrated supplements can cause GI upset, headache, rare allergic reaction, and may interact via cytochrome P450 enzymes.") :role (nl en "Stored in cell vacuoles; when tissue is chewed or damaged the enzyme myrosinase hydrolyses it to sulforaphane, an isothiocyanate of the mustard-oil-bomb defense that deters herbivores and insects and inhibits fungi and bacteria.")) (compound glucobrassicin :class glucosinolate-indole :cooking (nl en "Indole glucosinolates are reduced more than aliphatic ones by boiling, mainly by leaching into cooking water. Steaming retains more; overcooking reduces myrosinase-dependent I3C formation. I3C is acid-labile and converts to DIM in the stomach regardless of cooking.") :human (nl en "Benefit: I3C/DIM induce phase-II detox enzymes and shift estrogen metabolism toward 2-hydroxylation; studied for prevention of hormone-related cancers. Indole glucosinolates are a major fraction of broccoli glucosinolates; supplemental trial doses (300-400 mg/day I3C, 100-300 mg/day DIM) far exceed normal dietary intake. Harm: very high-dose I3C showed liver/thyroid changes and, in some animal models, tumor promotion; supplements may interact with drugs and hormone-sensitive conditions.") :role (nl en "Indole glucosinolate; myrosinase releases unstable intermediates that break down to indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and, in acid, to 3,3'-diindolylmethane (DIM), defending against herbivores and pathogens.")) (compound progoitrin :class glucosinolate-aliphatic :cooking (nl en "Cooking reduces goitrogenic activity: steaming to roughly one-third and boiling to roughly one-tenth of raw bioavailability; about 5 min boiling gives ~35% loss. Iodine-deficient people or those on thyroid medication should cook rather than eat very large raw quantities.") :human (nl en "Harm potential: goitrin competitively inhibits thyroid iodine uptake and hormone synthesis. Commercial broccoli contains <10 umol goitrin per 100 g serving, considered minimal risk in an iodine-sufficient diet. Documented hypothyroidism/myxedema cases involved ~1-1.5 kg/day raw crucifers (e.g. bok choy) for months, almost always with iodine deficiency. Benefit: none established at dietary levels.") :role (nl en "Aliphatic glucosinolate; myrosinase hydrolysis yields goitrin (5-vinyloxazolidine-2-thione), which blocks thyroid iodine uptake — an anti-feedant defense against herbivores.")) (compound kaempferol-glycosides :class flavonol-flavonoid :cooking (nl en "Water-soluble: steaming retains ~90% of flavonoids, boiling only ~20% (lost to the water), pressure cooking ~45%. Heat itself is less damaging than leaching, so use little water and short cooking.") :human (nl en "Benefit: dietary antioxidant and anti-inflammatory; associated with reduced chronic-disease risk. Typical florets ~0.2-13 mg/100 g fresh (average ~2.5-4 mg/100 g); leaves much higher (~27 mg/100 g). Harm: none at dietary levels; high supplemental doses carry theoretical pro-oxidant and drug-interaction risk.") :role (nl en "UV-screening antioxidants with antimicrobial and anti-herbivore activity; stored mainly as kaempferol 3-O-sophoroside, about 90% of broccoli flavonoids.")) (compound quercetin-glycosides :class flavonol-flavonoid :cooking (nl en "Same pattern as kaempferol: water-soluble, major loss by boiling into cooking water; steaming preserves most; short heat exposure has little effect.") :human (nl en "Benefit: antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and vascular-protective effects studied. Typical florets ~0.03-11 mg/100 g fresh (average ~0.5-2.5 mg/100 g); leaves higher (~9 mg/100 g). Harm: dietary amounts safe; supplements above ~1 g/day may cause headache, GI or kidney stress and inhibit some CYP enzymes (drug interactions).") :role (nl en "Flavonol antioxidant defending against UV damage, fungi and insect herbivory; co-occurs with kaempferol glycosides."))]} :src [(source :kind journal-review :uri "https://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/24/19/3593") (source :kind journal-review :uri "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/articles/10.3389/fnut.2020.00147/full") (source :kind journal-review :uri "https://digital.csic.es/bitstream/10261/391541/1/Multifaceted-Curieses_et_al-2025-Molecules.pdf") (source :kind journal-review :uri "https://academic.oup.com/nutritionreviews/article-pdf/74/4/248/5115599/nuv110.pdf") (source :kind journal-study :uri "https://europepmc.org/backend/ptpmcrender.fcgi?accid=PMC2722699&blobtype=pdf") (source :kind journal-study :uri "https://mitchell.ucdavis.edu/sites/g/files/dgvnsk916/files/2017-05/2010_Broccoli_Koh%20%2345.pdf") (source :kind database :uri "http://phenol-explorer.eu/reports/40") (source :kind reference :uri "https://www.mskcc.org/cancer-care/integrative-medicine/herbs/indole-3-carbinol") (source :kind government-report :uri "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK561029/?report=reader")])
